          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does the thyroid do, and what goes wrong in hypothyroidism?",
          "answer": "Thyroid hormone and hypothyroidism are explained below, with the sources named.\n\nThe thyroid is a gland in the neck. It mainly makes thyroxine, which the body converts into a more active hormone called triiodothyronine. Together these hormones set the metabolic rate, meaning how fast most of the body's tissues use energy. A small gland in the brain, the pituitary, watches the hormone level and releases a signal hormone (thyroid-stimulating hormone) to prompt the thyroid to make more. (Source: \"Hypothyroidism (underactive thyroid)\", general clinical reference)\n\nHypothyroidism means the thyroid does not make enough thyroid hormone. The guidance says that in most cases the problem is in the gland itself, which is called primary hypothyroidism. Because a failing thyroid makes the pituitary release more signal hormone, that signal level tends to rise before the thyroid hormone level falls out of range. This is why the signal hormone is usually the first test. (Source: same document)\n\nThe retrieved material did not cover the symptoms of hypothyroidism, how it is diagnosed in detail, or how it is treated. The passage it came from was also cut off partway through its discussion of the less common central form. If you have questions about your own situation, a clinician can talk through them with you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
